# Code conventions

## Context

The app compiles with TypeScript strict mode (`noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is on,
see `app/tsconfig.json`). Existing code (`app/src/reducer.ts`,
`app/src/actions.ts`, `app/src/selectors.ts`) uses named exports and immutable
updates; new code must match.

## Rule

- Named exports ONLY. NEVER use `export default`.
- NEVER use `any`, `@ts-ignore`, or `@ts-expect-error`. Fix the types instead.
- State updates are immutable: use spread, `map`, `filter` — NEVER mutate
  objects or arrays in place (no `task.done = ...`, no `tasks.push(...)`).
- File names are kebab-case (`text-utils.ts` style); types/interfaces are
  PascalCase.
- Do not add a linter/formatter config — lint is deliberately not configured
  in this sample.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rn "export default" app/src` — zero matches.
2. `grep -rnE "\bany\b|@ts-ignore|@ts-expect-error" app/src` — zero matches.
3. `cd app && npm run typecheck` passes.
4. In the reducer, every `case` returns a new object; no assignment to
   `state.*` anywhere.
